[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Architectural Patterns in iOS: MVC, MVVM, VIPER, and TCA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right architectural pattern is critical for scaling iOS applications. This tutorial explores four prominent patterns in the Swift ecosystem: MVC, MVVM, VIPER, and TCA (The Composable Architecture), analyzing their structures, trade-offs, and implementations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Model-View-Controller (MVC)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVC is the traditional Apple-backed architecture. It divides the application into three interconnected components. However, in iOS, it often leads to 'Massive View Controller' syndromes because UIViewController manages both view logic and controller logic."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import UIKit

class UserViewController: UIViewController {
    var user: User?
    private let nameLabel = UILabel()

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        updateUI()
    }

    func updateUI() {
        nameLabel.text = user?.name
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVVM introduces a ViewModel to decouple the View from the Model. The ViewModel prepares data for the UI and handles user interactions. In SwiftUI and modern UIKit (using Combine), MVVM is exceptionally popular due to reactive data binding."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import Foundation
import Combine

class UserViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var name: String = ""
    private var user: User

    init(user: User) {
        self.user = user
        self.name = user.name
    }

    func updateName(newString: String) {
        self.name = newString
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. VIPER"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"VIPER is a clean architecture approach standing for View, Interactor, Presenter, Entity, and Router. It segregates responsibilities strictly, making code highly testable but introducing significant boilerplate, which is heavy for small-to-medium apps."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"View: Displays UI and routes user inputs to the Presenter."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interactor: Contains business logic related to data or networking."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Presenter: Contains view logic, prepares data for display, and directs Interactor."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Entity: Plain data models."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Router: Handles navigation logic between modules."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. The Composable Architecture (TCA)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developed by Point-Free, TCA is a library for building applications in a consistent and understandable way, with composition, testing, and ergonomics in mind. It relies heavily on unidirectional data flow, reducers, and explicit state management."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"import ComposableArchitecture

struct CounterFeature: Reducer {
    struct State: Equatable {
        var count = 0
    }

    enum Action: Equatable {
        case decrementButtonTapped
        case incrementButtonTapped
    }

    func reduce(into state: inout State, action: Action) -> Effect<Action> {
        switch action {
        case .decrementButtonTapped:
            state.count -= 1
            return .none
        case .incrementButtonTapped:
            state.count += 1
            return .none
        }
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary & Comparison"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"MVC: Best for rapid prototyping and tiny apps; prone to massive files."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"MVVM: The industry standard for SwiftUI; balances testability and complexity."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"VIPER: Enterprise-grade modularity; extremely high boilerplate overhead."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TCA: Perfect for complex, state-heavy apps requiring absolute predictability and unit test isolation."}]
